Nexus Estate Web (.clinerules)
Overview

This repository contains the Next.js Web Application for the Nexus Estate platform.

The Web application is responsible only for:

User Interface (UI)
User Experience (UX)
Authentication integration
API consumption
State management
SEO
Accessibility
Client-side interactions

The Web application must never contain business logic.

All business rules belong to backend microservices.

The AI Agent must strictly follow every rule defined below.

1. Technology Stack

Always assume the project uses:

Next.js (App Router)
TypeScript (Strict Mode)
React 19+
Tailwind CSS
shadcn/ui
TanStack Query
React Hook Form
Zod
ESLint
Prettier

Never introduce alternative libraries without explicit instruction.

2. Contract-First Development
Single Source of Truth

All API contracts are defined inside:

shared-contracts/openapi/

The generated SDK:

@nexus-estate/typescript-sdk

is the only source of truth.

Never redefine:

DTOs
Interfaces
Response models
Request models
Enums

Never duplicate SDK types.

Missing Contract

If an API endpoint does not exist inside the SDK:

DO NOT:

create temporary interfaces
mock API structures
invent request bodies

Instead:

Update shared-contracts
Publish a new SDK version
Upgrade the SDK
Continue implementation

Contract-First development is mandatory.

3. Project Architecture

Use Feature-Based Architecture.

src/
│
├── app/
├── features/
│     ├── auth/
│     ├── property/
│     ├── booking/
│     └── profile/
│
├── components/
│
├── services/
│
├── hooks/
│
├── providers/
│
├── lib/
│
├── constants/
│
├── utils/
│
├── types/
│
└── styles/

Never organize code by file type inside features.

Each feature should own:

components
hooks
services
schemas
utils

when necessary.

4. App Router Standards

Always use App Router.

Prefer:

layout.tsx
loading.tsx
error.tsx
not-found.tsx
route.ts
template.tsx

Do not use the Pages Router unless explicitly required.

5. Server Components

Server Components are the default.

Only use Client Components when required for:

event handlers
browser APIs
state
effects
animations

Never add "use client" unnecessarily.

Prefer rendering on the server.

6. Data Fetching

Prefer:

Server Components
fetch()
Server Actions (when appropriate)

Use TanStack Query only for:

client state synchronization
mutations
cache management
polling
optimistic updates

Never fetch identical data twice.

Avoid unnecessary client-side requests.

7. API Layer

Never call APIs directly inside UI components.

Always use:

services/

Example:

features/property/services/property.service.ts

Components should never know API URLs.

8. SDK Usage

Always import request and response types from:

@nexus-estate/typescript-sdk

Never recreate:

interface Property

interface User

interface LoginRequest

interface LoginResponse

If a type exists in the SDK, reuse it.

9. State Management

Use:

TanStack Query → Server State

Use:

Zustand
or
React Context

for lightweight client state.

Never store server state inside Zustand.

Separate:

UI State
Server State
10. Forms

Always use:

React Hook Form
Zod

Validation inside the frontend exists only to improve UX.

Backend validation is always authoritative.

11. Authentication

Authentication is handled by backend services.

Frontend responsibilities:

login flow
logout flow
session persistence
token storage
refresh flow
route protection

Never:

verify JWT manually
trust user roles stored in localStorage
implement authorization rules belonging to backend
12. UI Components

Use:

shadcn/ui

Prefer:

reusable components
composition
accessibility
strongly typed props

Avoid:

huge components
duplicated UI

A component should have one responsibility.

13. Styling

Use Tailwind CSS.

Prefer:

utility classes
design tokens
CSS variables

Avoid:

inline styles
duplicated class strings
arbitrary values without reason
14. Performance

Always optimize:

bundle size
hydration
rendering
caching

Prefer:

next/image
next/font
dynamic imports
lazy loading

Memoization should only be added after identifying unnecessary renders.

Never optimize prematurely.

15. SEO

Every public page should define:

metadata
title
description
Open Graph
Twitter Card

Prefer server rendering for SEO-critical pages.

16. Accessibility

Every UI must support:

semantic HTML
keyboard navigation
screen readers
ARIA labels when required

Never sacrifice accessibility for appearance.

17. Error Handling

Every async page must handle:

Loading
Empty
Error
Success

Never swallow API errors.

Never expose backend stack traces.

Display meaningful messages.

18. Logging

Never use:

console.log
console.error
console.warn

for production code.

Use the project's logging utilities if available.

Remove debug logs before completion.

19. TypeScript

Strict Mode is mandatory.

Never use:

any

unless absolutely unavoidable.

Prefer:

unknown
generics
utility types
discriminated unions

Explicit typing is preferred over implicit assumptions.

20. Environment Variables

Never hardcode:

API URLs
Secrets
Keys

Always use environment variables.

Example:

NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL
21. Code Quality

Prefer:

SOLID
DRY
KISS
Composition
Small functions
Pure functions

Avoid:

duplicated logic
deeply nested conditions
magic strings
hardcoded values
22. Naming Conventions

Components:

PropertyCard.tsx

Hooks:

useProperties.ts

Services:

property.service.ts

Schemas:

property.schema.ts

Types:

Use SDK types whenever possible.

23. Testing Mindset

Generate code that is:

testable
modular
dependency-light

Avoid tightly coupled implementations.

24. AI Agent Responsibilities

The AI Agent must:

Follow existing project architecture.
Reuse SDK types.
Respect Contract-First development.
Prefer Server Components.
Keep Client Components minimal.
Generate production-ready code.
Optimize for readability and maintainability.
Follow Next.js best practices.
Keep components small and reusable.
Separate UI, business flow, and API communication.
Write clean, strongly typed TypeScript.
Produce scalable and consistent code.
25. AI Agent Must Never

Never:

invent API contracts
redefine SDK interfaces
duplicate request/response models
hardcode API responses
implement backend business logic
fetch data inside presentation components
use any
abuse useEffect
convert every component into "use client"
ignore loading/error states
bypass the service layer
hardcode URLs
introduce new libraries without approval
break the existing architecture
generate placeholder implementations that violate the shared contracts
Golden Rule

If the SDK already defines it, reuse it.

If the contract does not exist, update shared-contracts first.

The frontend is responsible for presentation and user interaction—not business logic.